<?xml version='1.0' encoding='UTF-8'?>
<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.7.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02451</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.7.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e190b8eb-026a-47bc-8460-7fd9ecaf2d9c</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><para id="para-00001">Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00002"><exercise id="_2cba9f5d-8a29-47f5-8c43-8db679437249" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00001"><para id="para-00002">Answer the following questions about the values of \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\) if this equation is written in standard form, \(ax^2+bx+c=0\).</para><para id="para-00003">Mai and Jada are solving the equation \(2x^2−7x=15\) using the quadratic formula.</para>
      <para id="para-00004">Enter the value of \(a\)?</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00001"><para id="para-00005">Correct! The value of \(a=2\).</para><para id="para-00006">Try again. Revisit activity 9.7.2.</para><para id="para-00007">The correct answer is 2. The value of \(a=2\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_7131c2b4-6b14-431e-8a5a-96bd8fb1a549" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00002"><para id="para-00008">Enter the value of \(b\)?</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00002"><para id="para-00009">Correct! The value of \(b=-7\).</para><para id="para-00010">Try again. Revisit activity 9.7.2.</para><para id="para-00011">The correct answer is -7. The value of \(b=-7\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_5133ff2f-3f56-4d89-8ce5-3060ad5f4c9f" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00003"><para id="para-00012">Enter the value of \(c\)?</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00003"><para id="para-00013">Correct! The value of \(c=-15\).</para><para id="para-00014">Try again. Revisit activity 9.7.2.</para><para id="para-00015">The correct answer is -15. The value of \(c=-15\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="b0d4e99e-7e04-410a-b900-79577a756d37" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00004"><para id="para-00016">Who has the correct solution?</para><para id="para-00017">Mai and Jada are solving the equation \(2x^2−7x=15\) using the quadratic formula but found different solutions.</para>
        
        <table class="os-raise-midsizetable" id="table-00001">
          <tgroup cols="2"><tbody><row>
              <entry><emphasis>Mai's Method</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>Jada's Method</emphasis></entry></row>
          <row>
              <entry>
                <para id="para-00018">
                  \(x=\frac{-7 \pm \sqrt{7^2-4(2)(-15)}}{2(2)}\) 
                  \(x=\frac{-7 \pm \sqrt{49-(-120)}}{4}\) 
                  \(x=\frac{-7 \pm \sqrt{169}}{4}\) 
                  \(x=\frac{-7 \pm 13}{4}\) 
                  \(x=\frac{-7+13}{4}=\frac{6}{4}=\frac{3}{2}\) 
                  \(x=\frac{-7-13}{4}=\frac{-20}{4}=-5\)
                </para>
              </entry>
              <entry>
                <para id="para-00019">
                  \(x=\frac{-(-7) \pm \sqrt{(-7)^2-4(2)(-15)}}{2(2)}\) 
                  \(x=\frac{7 \pm \sqrt{49-(-120)}}{4}\) 
                  \(x=\frac{7 \pm \sqrt{71}}{4}\)
                </para>
              </entry>
            </row>
          </tbody></tgroup></table>        
      </problem><solution id="sol-00004"><para id="para-00020">Correct! Mai used 7 for \(b\) instead of -7. For \(b^2\) in the formula, Jada computed \(-7^2\) instead of \((-7)^2\), so she wrote -49 instead of 49. </para><para id="para-00021">Try again. Revisit activities 9.7.2 – 9.7.4.</para><para id="para-00022">T The correct answer is: Neither one. Mai used 7 for \(b\) instead of -7. For \(b^2\) in the formula, Jada computed \(-7^2\) instead of \((-7)^2\), so she wrote -49 instead of 49. </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="a0b057d3-da9c-4de2-bf5e-2f14ace7e03a" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00005"><para id="para-00023">The equation \(h(t)=-16t^2+80t+64\) represents the height, in feet, of a potato \(t\) seconds after it was launched from a mechanical device.</para>
      <para id="para-00024">Choose the equation that would allow us to find the time the potato hits the ground.</para>
      <para id="para-00025">Multiple Choice:</para><para id="para-00026">\(0=16t^2+80t+64\)</para><para id="para-00027">\(0=-t^2-5t+4\)</para><para id="para-00028">\(0=-16t^2+80t+64\)</para><para id="para-00029">\(0= t^2-5t-4\)</para></problem><solution id="sol-00005"><para id="para-00030">Correct! \(0=-16t^2+80t+64\)</para><para id="para-00031">Try again. Revisit activities 9.7.2 – 9.7.4.</para><para id="para-00032">The correct answer is: \(0=-16t^2+80t+64\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="b6cc8061-e938-4bc1-9496-7e5fa4a806e0" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00006"><para id="para-00033">Enter the solution for the approximate time the potato hits the ground.</para><para id="para-00034">Solve the equation without graphing and write it down. Enter your solution for the approximate time the potato hits the ground rounded to one decimal place.</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00006"><para id="para-00035">Correct! \(t \approx 5.7\). Using the quadratic formula, with \(a=-16\), \(b=80\), and \(c=64\), the potato hits the ground about 5.7 seconds after being launched. (The negative solution has no meaning in this situation.)</para><para id="para-00036">Try again. Revisit activity 1.6.3.</para><para id="para-00037">The correct answer is \(t \approx 5.7\). Using the quadratic formula, with \(a=-16\), \(b=80\), and \(c=64\), the potato hits the ground about 5.7 seconds after being launched. (The negative solution has no meaning in this situation.) </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_6795296a-41c1-4a4b-b5ad-6da388418e4a" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00007"><para id="para-00038">Priya found \(x=3\) and \(x=-1\) as solutions to \(3x^2−6x−9=0\).</para>
      <para id="para-00039">If you check Priya's answer by substitution, is the answer correct?</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00007"><para id="para-00040">Correct! Yes. Substituting both 3 and -1 for \(x\) in the original quadratic expression and then evaluating the expression gives a value of 0.</para><para id="para-00041">Try again. Revisit activities 9.7.2 – 9.7.4.</para><para id="para-00042">The correct answer is Yes. Substituting each 3 and -1 for \(x\) in the original quadratic expression and then evaluating the expression gives a value of 0. </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_728d68b0-1c79-47ae-b653-ef30276a702c" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00008"><para id="para-00043">If you check Priya's answer by graphing, is the answer correct?</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00008"><para id="para-00044">Correct! Yes. Graphing the equation \(y=3x^2−6x−9\) shows \(x\)-intercepts at \((3,0)\) and \((-1,0)\).</para><para id="para-00045">Try again. Revisit activities 9.7.2 – 9.7.4.</para><para id="para-00046">The correct answer is Yes. Graphing the equation \(y=3x^2−6x−9\) shows \(x\)-intercepts at \((3,0)\) and \((-1,0)\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="eb766327-6e58-4440-b98c-78a41dd60fb2" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00009"><para id="para-00047">If you check Priya's answer by using the quadratic formula, is the answer correct?</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00009"><para id="para-00048">Correct! Yes. Substituting 3 for \(a\), -6 for \(b\), and -9 for \(c\) in the quadratic formula gives \(x=3\) and \(x=-1\).</para><para id="para-00049">Try again. Revisit activities 9.7.2 – 9.7.4.</para><para id="para-00050">The correct answer is Yes. Substituting 3 for \(a\), -6 for \(b\), and -9 for \(c\) in the quadratic formula gives \(x=3\) and \(x=-1\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="c4247392-6a1d-4e1e-8267-39f00abbceb2" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00010"><para id="para-00051">A frog jumps in the air. The height, in inches, of the frog is modeled by the function \(h(t)=60t−75t^2\), where \(t\) is the time after it jumped, measured in seconds.</para>
      <para id="para-00052"><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> solutions to the equation \(60t−75t^2=0\).</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00010"><para id="para-00053">Correct! \(t=0\), \(t=\frac{4}{5}\)</para><para id="para-00054">Try again. Revisit activity 9.7.3.</para><para id="para-00055">The correct answers are: \(t=0\), \(t=\frac{4}{5}\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="ee172b4a-d177-4f3f-bb23-dd918205ad05" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00011"><para id="para-00056">What do the solutions tell us about the jumping frog? <emphasis>Select two</emphasis> that apply.</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00011"><para id="para-00057">Correct! The frog landed \(\frac{4}{5}\) seconds after it jumped and was on the ground again. The height of the frog was 0 inches before it jumped.</para><para id="para-00058">Try again. Revisit activity 9.7.3.</para><para id="para-00059">The correct answers are: The frog landed \(\frac{4}{5}\) seconds after it jumped and was on the ground again. The height of the frog was 0 inches before it jumped.. </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_953362ca-c564-46a1-8d9a-20374b714c92" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00012"><para id="para-00060">A tennis ball is hit straight up in the air, and its height, in feet above the ground, is modeled by the equation \(f(t)=4+12t−16t^2\), where \(t\) is measured in seconds since the ball was thrown.</para>
      <para id="para-00061"><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> solutions to the equation \(0=4+12t−16t^2\).</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00012"><para id="para-00062">Correct! \(t=1\), \(t=-\frac{1}{4}\)</para><para id="para-00063">Try again. Revisit activity 9.7.3.</para><para id="para-00064">The correct answers are: \(t=1\), \(t=-\frac{1}{4}\). </para></solution></exercise><exercise id="_698e0e88-9992-422c-96c7-29aa023ebca8" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00013"><para id="para-00065">What do the solutions tell us about the tennis ball? <emphasis>Select two</emphasis> that apply.</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00013"><para id="para-00066">Correct! The negative solution doesn't have meaning because a negative value for time doesn't make sense. The ball hits the ground 1 second after it is hit.</para><para id="para-00067">Try again. Revisit activity 9.7.3.</para><para id="para-00068">The correct answers are: The negative solution doesn't have meaning because a negative value for time doesn't make sense. The ball hits the ground 1 second after it is hit. </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
